# Overview
#
# Passive voice makes the thing acted on the subject of the sentence
# (`the timeout can be configured`). The sentence names the actor after
# `by` or not at all (`the lock must be held by the caller`). Grammar
# calls the actor that a passive sentence names after `by` the agent.
#
# This tier reports a passive after a modal verb. A modal verb is a
# helping verb that marks possibility, ability, or obligation, such as
# `can`, `might`, or `must`. Such a passive hides who can or must act.
# The hidden actor is usually the reader or the caller, the person the
# sentence instructs. The fix names that actor (`you can configure the
# timeout`, `the caller must hold the lock`). The rule sits at
# Recommended, because a passive after a modal verb can also name a
# state or have no actor to name.
#
# Expression
#
# The rule matches a modal verb, `be`, and a participle (`retried`,
# `written`). A participle is the `-ing` or `-ed` form of a verb, such
# as `returning` or `cached`. An adverb or `not` can sit after the modal
# verb or after `be` (`must not be copied`, `can be safely removed`). An
# adverb is a word that describes a verb, an adjective, or a whole
# sentence, such as `safely` or `also`.
#
# `main` lists three forms after `be`: a verb form (`pp`), a `by` form,
# and an agent form (`ag`). Each form has two branches. One branch takes
# a modal verb right before `be` and leaves out `will`. The other branch
# takes a modal verb, then `not` or an adverb, then `be`, and it keeps
# `will`.
#
# The verb form is a verb, with or without an adverb or `not` before it.
# Harper's part-of-speech tagger marks each word with a tag for its word
# class. The filter `<VERB, !@stative>` requires one token to carry the
# tag `VERB` (a verb) and not to be one of the stative words. Stative
# words name a state instead of an action, and the tagger also marks
# them as verbs.
#
# The tagger marks many participles as adjectives (`cached`, `retried`,
# `flushed`), so `missed` lists the common ones by hand. The `!-` glued
# after the verb skips a hyphenated compound whose first half the tagger
# marks as a verb (`must be read-only`). The guard takes a punctuation
# mark too, so `the file can be skipped.` still counts.
#
# The `by` form is three words in a row: a word that the rule reads as
# the participle, `by`, and the word that opens the agent. It exists
# because the tagger misses many participles (`must be guarded by a
# lock`). The filter on the first word, `byform`, rejects every stative
# word. It also rejects every word that the tagger marks as an
# adjective, an adverb, a noun, a preposition, a determiner, a pronoun,
# or an exclamation. A preposition is a word that ties a noun to the
# rest of the sentence, such as `of`, `with`, or `by`. A determiner is a
# word that introduces a noun, such as `the`, `a`, or `this`.
#
# Weir has no token class for a number, so the word after `by` must be a
# pronoun, a proper noun, or a determiner instead. A proper noun is a
# name that takes a capital letter, such as `Kubernetes`.
#
# The agent form is a stative word, `by`, and an agent. Stative words
# still read as a passive when an agent follows them (`the flag can be
# set by the caller`), so `agentive` lists the stative words that take
# one. The agent must start with a determiner, a pronoun, or a proper
# noun, as in the `by` form, and the determiner must not open `by the
# time` or `by the way`.
#
# False Positives
#
# The stative list holds a progressive, a form of `be` plus an `-ing`
# verb (`must be running` names a state). It also holds the participles
# that docs use as adjectives (`must be set`, `can be enabled`) and the
# naming idioms (`can be called name`).
#
# A `by` that does not name an actor passes. The limit on the word after
# `by` skips a dimension (`64 by 64`) and the phrases `by default` and
# `by hand`.
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Optional ProsePassive reports every other passive, where naming
# the actor helps less often. It skips the word `be` after a modal verb,
# `not`, or an adverb, so the two tiers never report the same span.
#
# `will be` plus a participle stays out, because ProseFutureTense
# reports the `will` in it. The message of ProseFutureTense asks for the
# present tense with the actor as the subject (`the kubelet uses the
# default handler`), so one fix clears the sentence. ProseFutureTense
# skips `will not` and `will also`, so this rule reports those (`the
# file will not be read`). The roadmap words (`will be removed`) belong
# to ProseRoadmap.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 1840 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 18 of 20 sampled
# hits read clearer with the actor named (`you can pass values of this
# type`). The two misfires named a state (`might be uninitialized`) or
# had no actor to name (`the environment in which the command would be
# run`).
expr stative [PROG, missing, done, set, unset, enabled, disabled, required, needed, expected, allowed, supported, available, stuck, broken, gone, finished, lost, left, known, frozen, wedged, based, tied, built, made, fixed, live, called, named, meant, supposed, recommended, designed, intended, bound, referred, prepared, advised, assured, reminded, warned]
expr missed [cached, compiled, computed, configured, decoded, discarded, encoded, evicted, fetched, flushed, hashed, linted, normalized, parsed, patched, persisted, propagated, queued, redirected, reset, restarted, retried, revoked, rotated, sanitized, serialized, stripped, synced, tagged, truncated, validated]
expr verb [<VERB, !@stative>, @missed]
expr agentive [set, unset, enabled, disabled, required, needed, expected, allowed, supported, broken, known, built, made, fixed, called, named, designed, intended, bound, prepared, advised, assured, reminded, warned]
expr agent [(DET ![time, way]), PRON, PROPN]
expr modal [must, should, can, cannot, could, may, might, will, would, shall, can't, won't, shouldn't, couldn't, wouldn't, mustn't]
expr pp [@verb, ([ADV, not] @verb)]
expr ag [@agentive, ([ADV, not] @agentive)]
expr byform <!@stative, !ADJ, !ADV, !NOUN, !ADP, !DET, !PRON, !INTJ>
expr main [(<@modal, !will> be @byform by [DET, PRON, PROPN]), (@modal [not, ADV] be @byform by [DET, PRON, PROPN]), (<@modal, !will> be @pp!-), (@modal [not, ADV] be @pp!-), (<@modal, !will> be @ag by @agent), (@modal [not, ADV] be @ag by @agent)]
let message "Passive voice. Name who acts, such as `you can configure the timeout` or `the caller must hold the lock`."
let description "Flags a passive after a modal verb, such as `can be configured` or `must be held`, which hides who can or must act."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "You can configure the timeout."
allows "The job is retried."
allows "The cache might be stale."
allows "The flag will be removed in a future release."
allows "The flag will be added in a future release."
allows "The default handler will be used."
allows "The flag must be set."
allows "The flag can be enabled by default."
allows "The image can be 64 by 64 pixels."
allows "The lock must be read-only while the sweep runs."
allows "The job should be running by now."
